===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The study of the fate of the [[remains]] of organisms after they die, especially the study of [[fossilization]]. {{defdate|From 20th c.}}
#* '''2002''', Marcella H. Sorg, William D. Haglund, ''1: Advancing Forensic '''Taphonomy''': Purpose, Theory, and Process'', William D. Haglund, Marcella H. Sorg (editors), ''Advances in Forensic '''Taphonomy''''', CRC Press, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=5q1rg9Xl9CEC&pg=PA11&dq=%22taphonomy%22%7C%22taphonomies%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiR95H-4of9AhXqRLgEHW-0DwUQ6AF6BAhfEAI#v=onepage&q=%22taphonomy%22%7C%22taphonomies%22&f=false page 11],
#*: These characteristics are also applicable in forensic '''taphonomy'''. In contrast to our approach here, Buikstra and Ubelaker place "premortem and perimortem fractures, wounds, and abrasions" (p. 106) in the chapter on paleopathology, thus separating perimortem trauma from '''taphonomy'''.
#* '''2005''', ''Memoirs of {{w|Museums Victoria|Museum Victoria}}, Volume 62'', Museums Board of Victoria, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=d78rAQAAMAAJ&q=%22taphonomies%22&dq=%22taphonomies%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjpk7LF54f9AhUHTGwGHSROA34Q6AF6BAhgEAI page 10],
#*: Whilst there is some credit in distinguishing between '''taphonomies''' for thick-shelled forms and thin-shelled forms in shallower facies, these forms occur in both phacopids and homalonotids.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author={{w|Steven Pinker}}|title=The Better Angels of Our Nature|page=4|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012
|passage=Some cases may have an innocent explanation based in '''taphonomy''', the processes by which bodies are preserved over long spans of time.}}

